{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>The cucumbers and the melons.</b> They were disparaging the precious food they had in the manna, and in order to bring an evil report against it they would bring proof from the inferior fruits that they had for free. And it is known that there were fruits in abundance in the land of Egypt, as it is written (Genesis 13:10): ״כגן ה' כארץ מצרים״ — 'like the garden of HaShem, like the land of Egypt'; but they did not mention the important fruits, for the important ones were not given to them. And likewise what they mentioned — the דגה (fish) — for they would give them spoiled fish that had been caught four or five days earlier, for the important ones, which were alive and caught that very day, they would not give them. Therefore he did not say זכרנו את הדגים ('we remember the fish' [plural]) nor did he say זכרנו את הדג ('we remember the fish' [singular]), for wherever you find דגה it refers to spoiled fish, as from what is written (Exodus 7:21): ״והדגה אשר ביאור מתה ויבאש היאור״ — 'and the fish that were in the river died, and the river stank.' You also find in the prophet Jonah (Jonah 2:1): ״וימן ה' דג גדול״ — 'and HaShem appointed a great dag' — it first mentions it by the name דג (fish), and when it swallowed him and completed its count and died, it called it דגה, as it is written (ibid.): ״ויתפלל יונה אל ה' אלהיו ממעי הדגה״ — 'and Jonah prayed to HaShem his Elohim from the belly of the dagah.' And from this he said (ibid.): ״מבטן שאול שועתי״ — 'from the belly of Sheol I cried' — for one who sits in the belly of a dead thing is as one who sits in Sheol. And what it says afterward (ibid.): ״ויאמר ה' לדג ויקא וגו׳״ — 'and HaShem said to the dag, and it vomited, etc.' — this was a second dag, for it was not his dignity to be in the belly of a dead one; therefore they received a second fish that swallowed him and was commanded to vomit him onto dry land. And the upshot of the matter is that they despised the precious gift, and their entire intent was only to cast aspersion on the manna and bring an evil report against it; therefore he mentioned in the entire passage העם ('the people'), which is a term of disgrace. And this place is called קברות התאוה ('Graves of Craving'), and it is תבערה ('Taberah') that he mentioned, for in that very place where they complained and fire burned among them — there they craved a craving. And from this this passage of ותדבר מרים ('and Miriam spoke') is adjoined, for she brought an evil report against Moses; and adjoined to the passage of Miriam as well is the passage of the spies, who brought an evil report against the Land." "<b>And further,</b> one may say that the reason they mentioned these particular fruits indicates the evil of their nature and the strength of their craving. For the rabble, and also the great ones of the people who inclined after them and were drawn after cravings and bodily matters — and this is the doubling of expression to strengthen the matter, where it says התאוו תאוה ('they craved a craving'), that is, they drew craving forth from the desiring soul, and the trait of craving was strong in them in going after the lust of their heart in all kinds of bodily desires, as it says shortly afterward: בוכה למשפחותיו ('weeping for his families'), and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted it as concerning his families — concerning the forbidden sexual relations available to them. And they would not pay attention to the matters of the intellect above; this is what it says: ״בלתי אל המן עינינו״ — 'only the manna is before our eyes' — for since the manna was from a single element and was a simple substance, not composite like other foods, and it sharpened the intellect and brought a person to knowledge of intelligibles, therefore they despised and disparaged it and said ״בלתי אל המן עינינו״ — for this was contrary to their nature. And because of the strength of their craving and the lack of their intellect, they considered this great virtue to be a deficiency, for their evil nature blinded the eyes of their intellect in the pursuit of bodily matters. And from this they mentioned two categories of food that grow in the two bodily elements, which are water and earth, whose nature is to descend downward; this is what it says: ״זכרנו את הדגה את הקשואים״ וגו׳ — he mentioned fish from the element of water, and cucumbers and melons and the rest from the element of earth. Therefore he did not mention fruits of the tree, for they contain a portion of the element of air, but these — which are fruits of the ground and in which the element of earth is stronger. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in tractate Yoma, at the beginning of the chapter of Yom ha-Kippurim, that the reason he mentioned these fruits is because the manna would transform into the taste of all other kinds, but into the taste of these five it would not transform, because the taste of these five is harmful to pregnant and nursing women — like one who says to a woman, 'Do not eat such-and-such on account of the infant.'" "<b>And the Ramban of blessed memory wrote</b> concerning what it says about the fish being חנם (for free): that the fishermen would put them to work to haul fish from the nets, and they would give them fish for free, for such was their custom. Thus far. But our Rabbis of blessed memory said in tractate Yoma, at the beginning of the chapter of Yom ha-Kippurim: What is חנם? They would bring them from the public domain, as the Master said: the Holy One, blessed be He, would prepare fish for them in their vessels, as is found in tractate Sotah." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״אשר נאכל במצרים חנם״ — 'which we ate in Egypt for free.' If you say they were not given to them for free, it is written (Exodus 5:18): ״ותבן לא ינתן לכם״ — 'and straw shall not be given to you' — but fish were given to them for free! Rather, what is חנם? Free from the commandments. And the intention is that now they rebelled against the word of HaShem and rejected His service, and they came to cast off from themselves the yoke of His commandments, and they would recall their sojourn in Egypt when they were free men with respect to the commandments, as in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 4:34): ״גוי מקרב גוי״ — 'a nation from the midst of a nation' — for they were equal to the Egyptians, walking in their statutes and serving idolatry and growing the forelock like them."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:30.486253161Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}